A: Your test cases are not complete as you are just trying to enforce the contracts.


*

*Addition of elements: It should add to list, observe the index it was added, and the Count property should increase by 1 and the Contains method should show this added element.

*Removal of elements: It should remove from list (if present), observe the index it was removed from, and the Count property should decrease by 1 and the Contains method should now show its absence. 

*Insertion of elements: It should insert into list, observe the index it was inserted, and the Count property should increase by 1 and the Contains method should show this newly inserted element.


Unfortunately, due to the fact that Pex doesn't have built-in mechanism to handle collections and their properties, Pex is really good at mutations, but not at inspecting collections after mutation. It also doesn't understand the specific API for collections.
In this case, you might be better off writing your own unit tests with assertions and ensuring that your methods behave as they are supposed to. That would also give you more control over the specifics of the behavior in your tests.
If you wanted to stick with Pex, you could try using the API wrapper or Pex PexAPIWrapper and trying to inspect the internal state of Pex-managed collection. However, you would then be really working against the Pex's goal of its exploration-based generation, which is typically to find the easiest-to-find vulnerabilities.
Also note, Pex, like all mutation-based testing tools, does not guarantee to find all mistakes. This means that it may be blindly satisfied by finding just a small number of flaws, rather than identifying them all. So, while this test can help you identify likely errors, it might still fail in some edge cases.

A: Based on other answers here, you can run the mutation tests using the Pex Class Data. 

Mutation testing is a form of software testing where a mutant version of the original program is created by applying small changes to the original program. Mutants are then run against the tests for the original program. If the tests fail for the mutant, then it's considered a mutation. 

The steps to use Pex Class Data for mutation testing are:
1. Install Pex Class Data

2. Add this NuGet package to your project Test.csproj
<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Pex.ClassData" Version="1.0.0-beta-14325.2" />

3. Ensure you have unit tests in place.

4. Run PEX Class Data by:
   - Right click on the unit test project
   - Pick Add -> Pex Class Data

5. This will create a mutation testing project and will build them automatically. If builds fail, check the error log and fix any errors.

6. When the mutation testing projects finish building, you can review the results. Results will show for each mutation how it impacted the tests.

The Pex Class Data will find potential bugs and is quite effective at doing so. It will show when your tests fail or cover new branches in your code. They basically are like unit tests but for your code instead of finding logical errors.
You can read more about it here: https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dn432927.aspx
Keep in mind that Pex/ClassData tool is still in preview and it's designed to find a few bugs, not all of them, plus it may not work with every line of code. Also, it does not guarantee to find all mistakes, so be sure to do manual testing in certain areas.

A: My guess is that you are not performing the verification phase properly, as it seems you don't have any mechanism to check the post conditions in your contract methods.

However, note that there seems to be a misunderstanding. The Contract Verification Phase checks the pre and post condition of the method under test. Since Insert is a void method, it has no return value. So, you can't directly observe its return value during the Verification phase.

